1
00:00:04,166 --> 00:00:09,332
THE two gentlemen left Rosings the next morning; and Mr. Collins having been in waiting near the

2
00:00:09,332 --> 00:00:14,433
lodges, to make them his parting obeisance, was able to bring home the pleasing intelligence of

3
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:18,765
their appearing in very good health, and in as tolerable spirits as could be expected,

4
00:00:18,765 --> 00:00:25,400
after the melancholy scene so lately gone through at Rosings. To Rosings he then hastened to console

5
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:30,433
Lady Catherine and her daughter; and on his return brought back, with great satisfaction,

6
00:00:30,433 --> 00:00:36,200
a message from her Ladyship, importing that she felt herself so dull as to make her very

7
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:41,599
desirous of having them all to dine with her. Elizabeth could not see Lady Catherine without

8
00:00:41,599 --> 00:00:52,332
recollecting that, had she chosen it, she might by this time have been presented to her as her future niece; nor could she think, without a smile, of what her Ladyship’s indignation would have been.

9
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:58,099
“What would she have said? how would she have behaved?” were the questions

10
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:02,599
with which she amused herself. Their first subject was the diminution

11
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:16,633
of the Rosings’ party. “I assure you, I feel it exceedingly,” said Lady Catherine; “I believe nobody feels the loss of friends so much as I do. But I am particularly attached to

12
00:01:16,666 --> 00:01:29,065
these young men; and know them to be so much attached to me! They were excessively sorry to go! But so they always are. The dear Colonel rallied his spirits tolerably till just at last;

13
00:01:29,065 --> 00:01:43,400
but Darcy seemed to feel it most acutely—more, I think, than last year. His attachment to Rosings certainly increases.” Mr. Collins had a compliment and an allusion

14
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:47,500
to throw in here, which were kindly smiled on by the mother and daughter.

15
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:53,500
Lady Catherine observed, after dinner, that Miss Bennet seemed out of spirits; and immediately

16
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:59,366
accounting for it herself, by supposing that she did not like to go home again so soon, she added,—

17
00:01:59,366 --> 00:02:03,765
“But if that is the case, you must write to your mother to beg that you may stay

18
00:02:03,765 --> 00:02:08,000
a little longer. Mrs. Collins will be very glad of your company, I am sure.”

19
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:12,799
“I am much obliged to your Ladyship for your kind invitation,” replied Elizabeth;

20
00:02:12,799 --> 00:02:17,666
“but it is not in my power to accept it. I must be in town next Saturday.”

21
00:02:18,565 --> 00:02:25,466
“Why, at that rate, you will have been here only six weeks. I expected you to stay two months. I

22
00:02:25,466 --> 00:02:31,900
told Mrs. Collins so before you came. There can be no occasion for your going so soon. Mrs. Bennet

23
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:38,065
could certainly spare you for another fortnight.” “But my father cannot. He wrote last week

24
00:02:38,065 --> 00:02:39,299
to hurry my return.”

25
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:45,799
“Oh, your father, of course, may spare you, if your mother can. Daughters are never of

26
00:02:45,799 --> 00:02:50,733
so much consequence to a father. And if you will stay another month complete,

27
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:56,133
it will be in my power to take one of you as far as London, for I am going there early in June,

28
00:02:56,133 --> 00:02:59,666
for a week; and as Dawson does not object to the barouche-box,

29
00:02:59,666 --> 00:03:04,466
there will be very good room for one of you—and, indeed, if the weather should happen to be cool,

30
00:03:04,466 --> 00:03:08,799
I should not object to taking you both, as you are neither of you large.”

31
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:14,065
“You are all kindness, madam; but I believe we must abide by our original plan.”

32
00:03:14,799 --> 00:03:21,765
Lady Catherine seemed resigned. “Mrs. Collins, you must send a servant with them. You know I

33
00:03:21,765 --> 00:03:27,933
always speak my mind, and I cannot bear the idea of two young women travelling post by themselves.

34
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:35,433
It is highly improper. You must contrive to send somebody. I have the greatest dislike in the world

35
00:03:35,433 --> 00:03:41,966
to that sort of thing. Young women should always be properly guarded and attended, according to

36
00:03:41,966 --> 00:03:48,565
their situation in life. When my niece Georgiana went to Ramsgate last summer, I made a point of

37
00:03:48,565 --> 00:04:01,599
her having two men-servants go with her. Miss Darcy, the daughter of Mr. Darcy of Pemberley, and Lady Anne, could not have appeared with propriety in a different manner. I am excessively

38
00:04:01,599 --> 00:04:08,565
attentive to all those things. You must send John with the young ladies, Mrs. Collins. I am glad it

39
00:04:08,565 --> 00:04:13,532
occurred to me to mention it; for it would really be discreditable to you to let them go alone.”

40
00:04:14,565 --> 00:04:21,265
“My uncle is to send a servant for us.” “Oh! Your uncle! He keeps a man-servant,

41
00:04:21,265 --> 00:04:26,733
does he? I am very glad you have somebody who thinks of those things. Where shall you change

42
00:04:26,733 --> 00:04:32,366
horses? Oh, Bromley, of course. If you mention my name at the Bell, you will be attended to.”

43
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:41,900
Lady Catherine had many other questions to ask respecting their journey; and as she did not answer them all herself attention was necessary—which Elizabeth

44
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:46,532
believed to be lucky for her; or, with a mind so occupied, she might have forgotten

45
00:04:46,532 --> 00:04:51,799
where she was. Reflection must be reserved for solitary hours: whenever she was alone,

46
00:04:51,799 --> 00:05:01,633
she gave way to it as the greatest relief; and not a day went by without a solitary walk, in which she might indulge in all the delight of unpleasant recollections.

47
00:05:01,666 --> 00:05:12,200
Mr. Darcy’s letter she was in a fair way of soon knowing by heart. She studied every sentence; and her feelings towards its writer were at times widely different. When she remembered the style of

48
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:17,065
his address, she was still full of indignation: but when she considered how unjustly she had

49
00:05:17,065 --> 00:05:28,700
condemned and upbraided him, her anger was turned against herself; and his disappointed feelings became the object of compassion. His attachment excited gratitude, his general character respect:

50
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:34,532
but she could not approve him; nor could she for a moment repent her refusal, or feel the slightest

51
00:05:34,532 --> 00:05:40,166
inclination ever to see him again. In her own past behaviour, there was a constant source of

52
00:05:40,166 --> 00:05:46,933
vexation and regret: and in the unhappy defects of her family, a subject of yet heavier chagrin. They

53
00:05:46,933 --> 00:05:52,065
were hopeless of remedy. Her father, contented with laughing at them, would never exert himself

54
00:05:52,065 --> 00:05:56,966
to restrain the wild giddiness of his youngest daughters; and her mother, with manners so far

55
00:05:56,966 --> 00:06:03,265
from right herself, was entirely insensible of the evil. Elizabeth had frequently united with Jane in

56
00:06:03,265 --> 00:06:07,765
an endeavour to check the imprudence of Catherine and Lydia; but while they were supported by their

57
00:06:07,765 --> 00:06:13,466
mother’s indulgence, what chance could there be of improvement? Catherine, weak-spirited, irritable,

58
00:06:13,466 --> 00:06:19,233
and completely under Lydia’s guidance, had been always affronted by their advice; and Lydia,

59
00:06:19,233 --> 00:06:28,599
self-willed and careless, would scarcely give them a hearing. They were ignorant, idle, and vain. While there was an officer in Meryton, they would flirt with him;

60
00:06:28,599 --> 00:06:32,966
and while Meryton was within a walk of Longbourn, they would be going there for ever.

61
00:06:33,799 --> 00:06:38,866
Anxiety on Jane’s behalf was another prevailing concern; and Mr. Darcy’s explanation,

62
00:06:38,866 --> 00:06:48,866
by restoring Bingley to all her former good opinion, heightened the sense of what Jane had lost. His affection was proved to have been sincere, and his conduct cleared of all blame,

63
00:06:48,866 --> 00:06:54,433
unless any could attach to the implicitness of his confidence in his friend. How grievous then

64
00:06:54,433 --> 00:07:00,400
was the thought that, of a situation so desirable in every respect, so replete with advantage, so

65
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:06,200
promising for happiness, Jane had been deprived, by the folly and indecorum of her own family!

66
00:07:07,166 --> 00:07:10,832
When to these recollections was added the development of Wickham’s character,

67
00:07:10,832 --> 00:07:15,400
it may be easily believed that the happy spirits which had seldom been depressed before

68
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:20,733
were now so much affected as to make it almost impossible for her to appear tolerably cheerful.

69
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Their engagements at Rosings were as frequent during the last week of her stay as they had

70
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:31,166
been at first. The very last evening was spent there; and her Ladyship again inquired minutely

71
00:07:31,166 --> 00:07:35,565
into the particulars of their journey, gave them directions as to the best method of packing,

72
00:07:35,565 --> 00:07:40,900
and was so urgent on the necessity of placing gowns in the only right way, that Maria thought

73
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:46,366
herself obliged, on her return, to undo all the work of the morning, and pack her trunk afresh.

74
00:07:46,366 --> 00:07:51,500
When they parted, Lady Catherine, with great condescension, wished them a good journey,

75
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:54,265
and invited them to come to Hunsford again next year;

76
00:07:54,265 --> 00:07:59,565
and Miss de Bourgh exerted herself so far as to courtesy and hold out her hand to both.

77
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:10,733
CHAPTER XXXVIII.

78
00:08:10,733 --> 00:08:14,866
ON Saturday morning Elizabeth and Mr. Collins met for breakfast a few minutes before the

79
00:08:14,866 --> 00:08:19,099
others appeared; and he took the opportunity of paying the parting civilities which he

80
00:08:19,099 --> 00:08:21,000
deemed indispensably necessary.

81
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:27,200
“I know not, Miss Elizabeth,” said he, “whether Mrs. Collins has yet expressed

82
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:31,700
her sense of your kindness in coming to us; but I am very certain you will not leave the

83
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:35,200
house without receiving her thanks for it.

84
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:39,133
The favour of your company has been much felt, I assure you.

85
00:08:39,133 --> 00:08:43,866
We know how little there is to tempt anyone to our humble abode.

86
00:08:43,866 --> 00:08:49,500
Our plain manner of living, our small rooms, and few domestics, and the little we see of

87
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:55,299
the world, must make Hunsford extremely dull to a young lady like yourself; but I hope

88
00:08:55,299 --> 00:09:00,099
you will believe us grateful for the condescension, and that we have done everything in our power

89
00:09:00,099 --> 00:09:04,433
to prevent you spending your time unpleasantly.”

90
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:07,433
Elizabeth was eager with her thanks and assurances of happiness.

91
00:09:07,433 --> 00:09:11,700
She had spent six weeks with great enjoyment; and the pleasure of being with Charlotte,

92
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:16,133
and the kind attention she had received, must make her feel the obliged.

93
00:09:16,133 --> 00:09:20,166
Mr. Collins was gratified; and with a more smiling solemnity replied,—

94
00:09:20,166 --> 00:09:26,065
“It gives me the greatest pleasure to hear that you have passed your time not disagreeably.

95
00:09:26,065 --> 00:09:30,765
We have certainly done our best; and most fortunately having it in our power to introduce

96
00:09:30,765 --> 00:09:36,166
you to very superior society, and from our connection with Rosings, the frequent means

97
00:09:36,166 --> 00:09:41,633
of varying the humble home scene, I think we may flatter ourselves that your Hunsford

98
00:09:41,633 --> 00:09:45,265
visit cannot have been entirely irksome.

99
00:09:45,265 --> 00:09:50,000
Our situation with regard to Lady Catherine’s family is, indeed, the sort of extraordinary

100
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,733
advantage and blessing which few can boast.

101
00:09:53,733 --> 00:09:55,633
You see on what a footing we are.

102
00:09:55,633 --> 00:09:58,000
You see how continually we are engaged there.

103
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,765
In truth, I must acknowledge, that, with all the disadvantages of this humble parsonage,

104
00:10:03,765 --> 00:10:08,332
I should not think anyone abiding in it an object of compassion, while they are sharers

105
00:10:08,332 --> 00:10:11,565
of our intimacy at Rosings.”

106
00:10:11,565 --> 00:10:15,233
Words were insufficient for the elevation of his feelings; and he was obliged to walk

107
00:10:15,233 --> 00:10:19,900
about the room, while Elizabeth tried to unite civility and truth in a few short sentences.

108
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:25,765
“You may, in fact, carry a very favourable report of us into Hertfordshire, my dear cousin.

109
00:10:25,765 --> 00:10:29,733
I flatter myself, at least, that you will be able to do so.

110
00:10:29,733 --> 00:10:34,299
Lady Catherine’s great attentions to Mrs. Collins you have been a daily witness of;

111
00:10:34,299 --> 00:10:37,765
and altogether I trust it does not appear that your friend has drawn an unfortunate—but

112
00:10:37,765 --> 00:10:42,000
on this point it will be as well to be silent.

113
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Only let me assure you, my dear Miss Elizabeth, that I can from my heart most cordially wish

114
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,466
you equal felicity in marriage.

115
00:10:50,466 --> 00:10:54,966
My dear Charlotte and I have but one mind and one way of thinking.

116
00:10:54,966 --> 00:11:00,500
There is in everything a most remarkable resemblance of character and ideas between us.

117
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:04,700
We seem to have been designed for each other.”

118
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:08,565
Elizabeth could safely say that it was a great happiness where that was the case, and with

119
00:11:08,565 --> 00:11:13,966
equal sincerity could add, that she firmly believed and rejoiced in his domestic comforts.

120
00:11:13,966 --> 00:11:17,332
She was not sorry, however, to have the recital of them interrupted by the entrance of the

121
00:11:17,332 --> 00:11:18,599
lady from whom they sprang.

122
00:11:18,599 --> 00:11:19,633
Poor Charlotte!

123
00:11:19,633 --> 00:11:22,765
it was melancholy to leave her to such society!

124
00:11:22,765 --> 00:11:27,299
But she had chosen it with her eyes open; and though evidently regretting that her visitors

125
00:11:27,299 --> 00:11:30,832
were to go, she did not seem to ask for compassion.

126
00:11:30,832 --> 00:11:36,099
Her home and her housekeeping, her parish and her poultry, and all their dependent concerns,

127
00:11:36,099 --> 00:11:39,400
had not yet lost their charms.

128
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:44,099
At length the chaise arrived, the trunks were fastened on, the parcels placed within, and

129
00:11:44,099 --> 00:11:46,166
it was pronounced to be ready.

130
00:11:46,166 --> 00:11:50,065
After an affectionate parting between the friends, Elizabeth was attended to the carriage

131
00:11:50,065 --> 00:11:54,166
by Mr. Collins; and as they walked down the garden, he was commissioning her with his

132
00:11:54,166 --> 00:11:58,866
best respects to all her family, not forgetting his thanks for the kindness he had received

133
00:11:58,866 --> 00:12:03,966
at Longbourn in the winter, and his compliments to Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner, though unknown.

134
00:12:03,966 --> 00:12:08,466
He then handed her in, Maria followed, and the door was on the point of being closed,

135
00:12:08,466 --> 00:12:12,400
when he suddenly reminded them, with some consternation, that they had hitherto forgotten

136
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:14,532
to leave any message for the ladies of Rosings.

137
00:12:15,532 --> 00:12:17,832
“They had forgotten to leave any message” “But,” he added, “you will of course

138
00:12:17,832 --> 00:12:22,265
wish to have your humble respects delivered to them, with your grateful thanks for their

139
00:12:22,265 --> 00:12:25,265
kindness to you while you have been here.”

140
00:12:25,265 --> 00:12:28,832
Elizabeth made no objection: the door was then allowed to be shut, and the carriage

141
00:12:28,832 --> 00:12:29,933
drove off.

142
00:12:29,933 --> 00:12:31,900
“Good gracious!”

143
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:36,599
cried Maria, after a few minutes’ silence, “it seems but a day or two since we first

144
00:12:36,599 --> 00:12:39,332
came! and yet how many things have happened!”

145
00:12:39,332 --> 00:12:43,332
“A great many indeed,” said her companion, with a sigh.

146
00:12:43,332 --> 00:12:47,866
“We have dined nine times at Rosings, besides drinking tea there twice!

147
00:12:47,866 --> 00:12:50,433
How much I shall have to tell!”

148
00:12:50,433 --> 00:12:55,633
Elizabeth privately added, “And how much I shall have to conceal!”

149
00:12:55,633 --> 00:13:00,166
Their journey was performed without much conversation, or any alarm; and within four hours of their

150
00:13:00,166 --> 00:13:05,233
leaving Hunsford they reached Mr. Gardiner’s house, where they were to remain a few days.

151
00:13:05,233 --> 00:13:10,166
Jane looked well, and Elizabeth had little opportunity of studying her spirits, amidst

152
00:13:10,166 --> 00:13:14,032
the various engagements which the kindness of her aunt had reserved for them.

153
00:13:14,032 --> 00:13:19,200
But Jane was to go home with her, and at Longbourn there would be leisure enough for observation.

154
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:23,133
It was not without an effort, meanwhile, that she could wait even for Longbourn, before

155
00:13:23,133 --> 00:13:26,299
she told her sister of Mr. Darcy’s proposals.

156
00:13:26,299 --> 00:13:31,032
To know that she had the power of revealing what would so exceedingly astonish Jane, and

157
00:13:31,032 --> 00:13:35,265
must, at the same time, so highly gratify whatever of her own vanity she had not yet

158
00:13:35,265 --> 00:13:39,666
been able to reason away, was such a temptation to openness as nothing could have conquered,

159
00:13:39,666 --> 00:13:44,332
but the state of indecision in which she remained as to the extent of what she should communicate,

160
00:13:44,332 --> 00:13:48,532
and her fear, if she once entered on the subject, of being hurried into repeating something

161
00:13:48,532 --> 00:13:51,900
of Bingley, which might only grieve her sister further.

162
00:13:53,832 --> 00:13:59,666
“How nicely we are crammed in.”

163
00:13:59,666 --> 00:14:01,599
CHAPTER XXXIX.

164
00:14:01,599 --> 00:14:06,032
IT was the second week in May, in which the three young ladies set out together from Gracechurch

165
00:14:06,032 --> 00:14:10,765
Street for the town of ——, in Hertfordshire; and, as they drew near the appointed inn where

166
00:14:10,765 --> 00:14:15,599
Mr. Bennet’s carriage was to meet them, they quickly perceived, in token of the coachman’s

167
00:14:15,599 --> 00:14:19,933
punctuality, both Kitty and Lydia looking out of a dining-room upstairs.

168
00:14:19,933 --> 00:14:24,000
These two girls had been above an hour in the place, happily employed in visiting an

169
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:29,832
opposite milliner, watching the sentinel on guard, and dressing a salad and cucumber.

170
00:14:29,832 --> 00:14:34,200
After welcoming their sisters, they triumphantly displayed a table set out with such cold meat

171
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:40,099
as an inn larder usually affords, exclaiming, “Is not this nice? is not this an agreeable

172
00:14:40,099 --> 00:14:41,099
surprise?”

173
00:14:41,099 --> 00:14:45,332
“And we mean to treat you all,” added Lydia; “but you must lend us the money,

174
00:14:45,332 --> 00:14:47,733
for we have just spent ours at the shop out there.”

175
00:14:47,733 --> 00:14:51,400
Then showing her purchases,—“Look here, I have bought this bonnet.

176
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:54,832
I do not think it is very pretty; but I thought I might as well buy it as not.

177
00:14:54,832 --> 00:14:59,900
I shall pull it to pieces as soon as I get home, and see if I can make it up any better.”

178
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:04,133
And when her sisters abused it as ugly, she added, with perfect unconcern, “Oh, but

179
00:15:04,133 --> 00:15:08,466
there were two or three much uglier in the shop; and when I have bought some prettier-coloured

180
00:15:08,466 --> 00:15:11,765
satin to trim it with fresh, I think it will be very tolerable.

181
00:15:11,765 --> 00:15:16,332
Besides, it will not much signify what one wears this summer, after the ——shire have

182
00:15:16,332 --> 00:15:18,966
left Meryton, and they are going in a fortnight.”

183
00:15:18,966 --> 00:15:20,733
“Are they, indeed?”

184
00:15:20,733 --> 00:15:23,099
cried Elizabeth, with the greatest satisfaction.

185
00:15:23,099 --> 00:15:28,433
“They are going to be encamped near Brighton; and I do so want papa to take us all there

186
00:15:28,433 --> 00:15:29,565
for the summer!

187
00:15:29,565 --> 00:15:33,065
It would be such a delicious scheme, and I dare say would hardly cost anything at all.

188
00:15:33,065 --> 00:15:35,466
Mamma would like to go, too, of all things!

189
00:15:35,466 --> 00:15:38,133
Only think what a miserable summer else we shall have!”

190
00:15:38,133 --> 00:15:43,332
“Yes,” thought Elizabeth; “that would be a delightful scheme, indeed, and completely

191
00:15:43,332 --> 00:15:44,900
do for us at once.

192
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:45,900
Good Heaven!

193
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:51,000
Brighton and a whole campful of soldiers, to us, who have been overset already by one

194
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,700
poor regiment of militia, and the monthly balls of Meryton!”

195
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:59,265
“Now I have got some news for you,” said Lydia, as they sat down to table.

196
00:15:59,265 --> 00:16:00,366
“What do you think?

197
00:16:00,366 --> 00:16:06,700
It is excellent news, capital news, and about a certain person that we all like.”

198
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:11,433
Jane and Elizabeth looked at each other, and the waiter was told that he need not stay.

199
00:16:11,433 --> 00:16:15,299
Lydia laughed, and said,— “Ay, that is just like your formality and

200
00:16:15,299 --> 00:16:16,299
discretion.

201
00:16:16,299 --> 00:16:18,799
You thought the waiter must not hear, as if he cared!

202
00:16:18,799 --> 00:16:21,933
I dare say he often hears worse things said than I am going to say.

203
00:16:21,933 --> 00:16:23,366
But he is an ugly fellow!

204
00:16:23,366 --> 00:16:24,500
I am glad he is gone.

205
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:26,599
I never saw such a long chin in my life.

206
00:16:26,599 --> 00:16:32,933
Well, but now for my news: it is about dear Wickham; too good for the waiter, is not it?

207
00:16:32,933 --> 00:16:37,032
There is no danger of Wickham’s marrying Mary King—there’s for you!

208
00:16:37,032 --> 00:16:40,200
She is gone down to her uncle at Liverpool; gone to stay.

209
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:42,233
Wickham is safe.”

210
00:16:42,233 --> 00:16:48,265
“And Mary King is safe!” added Elizabeth; “safe from a connection imprudent as to

211
00:16:48,265 --> 00:16:49,265
fortune.”

212
00:16:49,265 --> 00:16:51,933
“She is a great fool for going away, if she liked him.”

213
00:16:51,933 --> 00:16:56,233
“But I hope there is no strong attachment on either side,” said Jane.

214
00:16:56,233 --> 00:16:57,966
“I am sure there is not on his.

215
00:16:57,966 --> 00:17:01,299
I will answer for it, he never cared three straws about her.

216
00:17:01,299 --> 00:17:05,265
Who could about such a nasty little freckled thing?”

217
00:17:05,265 --> 00:17:09,366
Elizabeth was shocked to think that, however incapable of such coarseness of expression

218
00:17:09,366 --> 00:17:13,133
herself, the coarseness of the sentiment was little other than her own breast had formerly

219
00:17:13,133 --> 00:17:15,532
harboured and fancied liberal!

220
00:17:15,532 --> 00:17:19,933
As soon as all had ate, and the elder ones paid, the carriage was ordered; and, after

221
00:17:19,933 --> 00:17:24,633
some contrivance, the whole party, with all their boxes, workbags, and parcels, and the

222
00:17:24,633 --> 00:17:28,433
unwelcome addition of Kitty’s and Lydia’s purchases, were seated in it.

223
00:17:28,433 --> 00:17:30,565
“How nicely we are crammed in!”

224
00:17:30,565 --> 00:17:31,565
cried Lydia.

225
00:17:31,565 --> 00:17:35,200
“I am glad I brought my bonnet, if it is only for the fun of having another band-box!

226
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:40,233
Well, now let us be quite comfortable and snug, and talk and laugh all the way home.

227
00:17:40,233 --> 00:17:43,433
And in the first place, let us hear what has happened to you all since you went away.

228
00:17:43,433 --> 00:17:44,832
Have you seen any pleasant men?

229
00:17:44,832 --> 00:17:46,565
Have you had any flirting?

230
00:17:46,565 --> 00:17:49,733
I was in great hopes that one of you would have got a husband before you came back.

231
00:17:49,733 --> 00:17:52,666
Jane will be quite an old maid soon, I declare.

232
00:17:52,666 --> 00:17:54,265
She is almost three-and-twenty!

233
00:17:54,265 --> 00:17:55,265
Lord!

234
00:17:55,265 --> 00:17:57,799
how ashamed I should be of not being married before three-and-twenty!

235
00:17:57,799 --> 00:18:02,233
My aunt Philips wants you so to get husbands you can’t think.

236
00:18:02,233 --> 00:18:06,233
She says Lizzy had better have taken Mr. Collins; but I do not think there would have been any

237
00:18:06,233 --> 00:18:07,466
fun in it.

238
00:18:07,466 --> 00:18:08,466
Lord!

239
00:18:08,466 --> 00:18:11,866
how I should like to be married before any of you! and then I would chaperon you about

240
00:18:11,866 --> 00:18:12,933
to all the balls.

241
00:18:12,933 --> 00:18:13,933
Dear me!

242
00:18:13,933 --> 00:18:16,299
we had such a good piece of fun the other day at Colonel Forster’s!

243
00:18:16,299 --> 00:18:20,332
Kitty and me were to spend the day there, and Mrs. Forster promised to have a little

244
00:18:20,332 --> 00:18:24,866
dance in the evening; (by-the-bye, Mrs. Forster and me are such friends!) and so she asked

245
00:18:24,866 --> 00:18:29,166
the two Harringtons to come: but Harriet was ill, and so Pen was forced to come by herself;

246
00:18:29,166 --> 00:18:30,933
and then, what do you think we did?

247
00:18:30,933 --> 00:18:35,799
We dressed up Chamberlayne in woman’s clothes, on purpose to pass for a lady,—only think

248
00:18:35,799 --> 00:18:36,799
what fun!

249
00:18:36,799 --> 00:18:41,099
Not a soul knew of it, but Colonel and Mrs. Forster, and Kitty and me, except my aunt,

250
00:18:41,099 --> 00:18:45,466
for we were forced to borrow one of her gowns; and you cannot imagine how well he looked!

251
00:18:45,466 --> 00:18:49,633
When Denny, and Wickham, and Pratt, and two or three more of the men came in, they did

252
00:18:49,633 --> 00:18:51,433
not know him in the least.

253
00:18:51,433 --> 00:18:52,433
Lord!

254
00:18:52,433 --> 00:18:53,433
how I laughed!

255
00:18:53,433 --> 00:18:54,532
and so did Mrs. Forster.

256
00:18:54,532 --> 00:18:55,933
I thought I should have died.

257
00:18:55,933 --> 00:18:59,900
And that made the men suspect something, and then they soon found out what was the matter.”

258
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:06,366
With such kind of histories of their parties and good jokes did Lydia, assisted by Kitty’s

259
00:19:06,366 --> 00:19:11,099
hints and additions, endeavour to amuse her companions all the way to Longbourn.

260
00:19:11,099 --> 00:19:14,765
Elizabeth listened as little as she could, but there was no escaping the frequent mention

261
00:19:14,765 --> 00:19:16,799
of Wickham’s name.

262
00:19:16,799 --> 00:19:19,000
Their reception at home was most kind.

263
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,565
Mrs. Bennet rejoiced to see Jane in undiminished beauty; and more than once during dinner did

264
00:19:23,565 --> 00:19:29,366
Mr. Bennet say voluntarily to Elizabeth,—— “I am glad you are come back, Lizzy.”

265
00:19:29,366 --> 00:19:33,265
Their party in the dining-room was large, for almost all the Lucases came to meet Maria

266
00:19:33,265 --> 00:19:38,466
and hear the news; and various were the subjects which occupied them: Lady Lucas was inquiring

267
00:19:38,466 --> 00:19:42,532
of Maria, across the table, after the welfare and poultry of her eldest daughter; Mrs. Bennet

268
00:19:42,532 --> 00:19:47,000
was doubly engaged, on one hand collecting an account of the present fashions from Jane,

269
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,900
who sat some way below her, and on the other, retailing them all to the younger Miss Lucases;

270
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:56,032
and Lydia, in a voice rather louder than any other person’s, was enumerating the various

271
00:19:56,032 --> 00:19:58,500
pleasures of the morning to anybody who would hear her.

272
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:03,332
“Oh, Mary,” said she, “I wish you had gone with us, for we had such fun!

273
00:20:03,332 --> 00:20:07,065
as we went along Kitty and me drew up all the blinds, and pretended there was nobody

274
00:20:07,065 --> 00:20:11,400
in the coach; and I should have gone so all the way, if Kitty had not been sick; and when

275
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:14,633
we got to the George, I do think we behaved very handsomely, for we treated the other

276
00:20:14,633 --> 00:20:18,200
three with the nicest cold luncheon in the world, and if you would have gone, we would

277
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:19,500
have treated you too.

278
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:21,666
And then when we came away it was such fun!

279
00:20:21,666 --> 00:20:23,400
I thought we never should have got into the coach.

280
00:20:23,400 --> 00:20:26,065
I was ready to die of laughter.

281
00:20:26,065 --> 00:20:28,000
And then we were so merry all the way home!

282
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:32,933
we talked and laughed so loud, that anybody might have heard us ten miles off!”

283
00:20:32,933 --> 00:20:38,733
To this, Mary very gravely replied, “Far be it from me, my dear sister, to depreciate

284
00:20:38,733 --> 00:20:39,966
such pleasures.

285
00:20:39,966 --> 00:20:43,433
They would doubtless be congenial with the generality of female minds.

286
00:20:43,433 --> 00:20:45,700
But I confess they would have no charms for me.

287
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:48,565
I should infinitely prefer a book.”

288
00:20:48,565 --> 00:20:50,933
But of this answer Lydia heard not a word.

289
00:20:50,933 --> 00:20:54,966
She seldom listened to anybody for more than half a minute, and never attended to Mary

290
00:20:54,966 --> 00:20:55,966
at all.

291
00:20:55,966 --> 00:21:00,099
In the afternoon Lydia was urgent with the rest of the girls to walk to Meryton, and

292
00:21:00,099 --> 00:21:04,000
see how everybody went on; but Elizabeth steadily opposed the scheme.

293
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,532
It should not be said, that the Miss Bennets could not be at home half a day before they

294
00:21:07,532 --> 00:21:09,000
were in pursuit of the officers.

295
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,065
There was another reason, too, for her opposition.

296
00:21:11,065 --> 00:21:16,565
She dreaded seeing Wickham again, and was resolved to avoid it as long as possible.

297
00:21:16,565 --> 00:21:20,966
The comfort to her, of the regiment’s approaching removal, was indeed beyond expression.

298
00:21:20,966 --> 00:21:25,799
In a fortnight they were to go, and once gone, she hoped there could be nothing more to plague

299
00:21:25,799 --> 00:21:28,000
her on his account.

300
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:31,500
She had not been many hours at home, before she found that the Brighton scheme, of which

301
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:36,032
Lydia had given them a hint at the inn, was under frequent discussion between her parents.

302
00:21:36,032 --> 00:21:40,433
Elizabeth saw directly that her father had not the smallest intention of yielding; but

303
00:21:40,433 --> 00:21:44,765
his answers were at the same time so vague and equivocal, that her mother, though often

304
00:21:44,765 --> 00:21:56,000
disheartened, had never yet despaired of succeeding at last.
